⏺ Every action is now limited to the user in the X-User-ID header, and POST 
  /users/ works without the header. All 51 tests pass.

  Access rules (all of them are my choices; easy to change):
  - Meetings: each meeting has a new owner_id, set to whoever created it, and
    it's included in the API response. Owners and attendees can see and update a
    meeting. Only the owner can delete it; an attendee who tries gets 403.
  - Notes and tasks: you can access them only if you can access their meeting.
    Creating one on someone else's meeting, or moving a task to one,
    returns 404.
  - Users: you see yourself and anyone you share a meeting with. You can still
    add any existing user id as an attendee; otherwise you couldn't invite
    anyone.
  - Hidden records return 404, not 403, so nobody can tell whether something
    they can't see exists.

  How it's enforced:
  - mesh.py: get_mesh now returns 401 if the header names a user who doesn't
    exist. A new get_anonymous_mesh (with no user) is used only by POST /users/.
  - Meetings do the filtering themselves, with a single query for "meetings I
    own or attend".
  - Notes, tasks and users ask the meeting service through the mesh which
    meetings the user can access. There's a new mesh.meetings.accessible_ids()
    for this. So the "who can see what" rule lives only in MeetingService.

  Tests: a new tests/test_access.py uses two users and checks that an outsider
  can't read, change, delete or add to another user's meeting, notes or tasks.
  It also covers attendee access, the owner-only delete, and which users each
  person can see. I fixed two existing tests that assumed the old behaviour:
  /users/1 is now the requesting user, so it's found, and a newly created user
  is no longer visible to someone who shares no meeting with them.

  Schema change: meetings has a new owner_id column, and the startup code won't
  add a column to a table that already exists. There's no meetings.db right now,
  so you're fine. If one exists from an older version, delete it before
  starting the server.

