  Ran 1 shell command (ctrl+o to expand)

⏺ The mesh now caches reads for each user and clears them when data changes. All
  65 tests pass. I also broke the cache on purpose twice: dropping the declared
  reads on meetings.get made 2 tests fail, and turning off the tracking of
  nested calls made 3 fail, so the invalidation tests do catch mistakes.

  How it fits together
  - cache.py (new):
    - The @cached decorator marks a service method as a read. reads=(...) lists
      services whose data the method reads straight from the database.
    - CacheBackend is the storage interface, and InMemoryCache is the local
      version.
  - mesh.py: a new CachingTransport wraps the in-process transport.
    build_local_mesh turns it on by default with one shared in-memory cache, and
    cache=None turns it off.
  - Services: every get/list method and meetings.accessible_ids is marked
    @cached. meetings.get declares that it reads users, notes and tasks, because
    a meeting's details include them. meetings.list declares users, for the
    attendees.

  How it behaves
  - Lifetime: entries are keyed by user, service, method and arguments, and they
    last across requests until a write clears them.
  - Invalidation: any call not marked @cached counts as a write. It clears, for
    every user, all entries that read the service it wrote to. Example: when
    Alice renames a meeting, Bob's cached copy of it is cleared.
  - Dependencies: a cached read that calls another service through the mesh is
    tracked automatically. Example: users.list calls meetings.list, so creating
    a meeting clears every user's cached users.list.
  - Errors aren't cached.
  - Cached values are copies, so code that changes a returned object can't alter
    what's stored.
  - Races: a read that overlaps with a write won't store its possibly
    out-of-date result.

  Tests: tests/test_cache.py checks cache hits, entries kept separate per user,
  each kind of invalidation (same service, declared reads, nested calls, other
  users' entries), copying of values, and the race guard. An autouse fixture in
  conftest.py empties the shared cache between tests, since each test gets a
  fresh database where the same ids repeat.

  Limits:
  - Not shared between processes. If you run uvicorn with several workers, each
    worker has its own cache, and a write in one won't clear the others, so
    they'd serve out-of-date data. You'd need a shared backend such as Redis;
    the CacheBackend interface is the place to add one.
  - No size limit or expiry. Entries stay until something invalidates them.
  - Coarse invalidation. A write clears everything that reads that service, not
    just the affected records. That's simpler and safe, but it throws away more
    than it needs to.
  - Changes made outside the mesh aren't noticed, for example editing
    meetings.db by hand.

  Next on the list is the get_tools() decorator. It will reuse the same pattern
  of marking service methods.

