⏺ Services can now be exposed as LLM tools: mesh.get_tools() returns tool
  definitions for the model, and mesh.call_tool() runs the calls it sends back.
  All 78 tests pass. I haven't sent the definitions to the real Anthropic API,
  so whether it accepts them is untested.

  What's new
  - tools.py:
    - @expose_tools is the class decorator. With no arguments it exposes the
      CRUD methods the service defines (create, list, get, update, delete), and
      @expose_tools(methods=[...]) picks them explicitly.
    - collect_tools() gathers the tools from all decorated services.
  - Services: all four are decorated, giving 16 tools named like
    meetings_update, one per HTTP endpoint. Internal helpers like accessible_ids
    stay hidden.
  - Docstrings: each exposed method now has one written for the LLM, including
    the access rules (for example, "only the meeting's owner can delete it").
    The decorator raises an error if an exposed method has no docstring.
  - mesh.py:
    - mesh.get_tools() returns {name, description, input_schema} for each tool,
      the format the Anthropic Messages API expects.
    - mesh.call_tool(name, input) validates the model's input, runs the method
      through the mesh, and returns a JSON-ready result.

  How the tool input is built
  - Flattened request models: a parameter typed as a request model (like
    MeetingUpdate) has its fields pulled up to the top level, so the model sees
    meetings_update(meeting_id, title?, description?, ...) with no nested
    objects. call_tool rebuilds the model from the flat input.
  - Partial updates still work: a field the LLM leaves out is left unchanged,
    the same as with PUT.
  - Name clashes: if a parameter and a model field would end up with the same
    name, the decorator raises an error.
  - Access rules apply: because call_tool goes through the mesh, the
    user-specific access checks and the cache apply exactly as they do for HTTP
    requests. An LLM acting for Bob can't read Alice's meetings.

  Tests: tests/test_tools.py checks the tool list and format, the flattened
  schemas, a create-and-read round trip, partial updates, per-user access,
  invalid input, unknown tools, and the decorator's own rules (methods=, missing
  docstring, name clashes).

  Worth knowing:
  - call_tool passes errors through as-is: NotFoundError, ValidationError,
    KeyError for an unknown tool. An agent loop would catch these and send them
    back to the model as tool_result with is_error: true.
  - users_create is exposed too, so an LLM could create users. If you don't want
    that, I can remove it with methods= on UserService.

  From the README list, what's left is making the transport configurable (local
  vs. cloud), plus the small code issues and the README updates at the end.

