⏺ You can now choose where data is stored with the MESH_BACKEND environment
  variable:

  uvicorn main:app --reload                       # default: sqlite, the 
  meetings.db file
  MESH_BACKEND=memory uvicorn main:app --reload   # in-memory database, wiped on
  restart

  What changed
  - database.py:
    - get_backend() reads MESH_BACKEND and raises an error for anything other
      than sqlite or memory, so a typo stops the app at startup instead of
      quietly using the wrong database.
    - create_db_engine(backend) builds the database engine for the chosen
      option. The in-memory one uses a single shared connection, so every
      request sees the same data.
    - Nothing else changed: services, mesh, cache and tools are untouched, and
      the tables are still created at startup.
  - tests/conftest.py now builds its test database with
    create_db_engine("memory") instead of its own copy of the setup.
  - tests/test_database.py (new) checks the default, reading the variable,
    rejecting unknown values, the file path for sqlite, and that memory keeps
    data across sessions.

  Checks: all 84 tests pass. I also ran a real server with MESH_BACKEND=memory:
  a user I created could be read back, was gone after a restart, and meetings.db
  was never touched.

  Things to know:
  - This switches the storage, not the transport. Calls still go through
    LocalTransport, so the Transport interface remains the place to add remote
    calls later.
  - With --reload, every code change restarts the server, which wipes the
    in-memory database.
  - The in-memory database uses a single connection shared by all threads.
    That's fine for local development but not for heavy concurrent use.

  Still on the list: the small code issues (attendees loaded without going
  through the mesh, free-text task status, deprecation warnings) and the README
  at the end.

