⏺ All 98 pass. I'll confirm the session test really catches a half-created
  meeting being left behind, by temporarily moving the check after the meeting
  is written to the session:

  Ran 1 shell command (ctrl+o to expand)

⏺ Attendee checks now go through the mesh, and MeetingService no longer
  references the users model at all. All 98 tests pass with no warnings. When I
  deliberately moved the check after the meeting is written to the session, the
  new session test failed, so it does catch that mistake.

  What changed
  - services/users.py: a new missing_ids(user_ids) method returns the ids that
    don't exist.
    - It deliberately ignores the visibility rule, because you can invite any
      existing user.
    - It isn't exposed as an LLM tool.
    - It isn't cached. It's cheap, and its list argument can't be used as a
      cache key.
  - services/meetings.py:
    - _load_attendees is replaced by _check_attendees, which validates the ids
      through mesh.users.missing_ids and removes duplicates.
    - A new _set_attendees writes the meeting_users rows directly; that table
      belongs to meetings.
    - _accessible() now finds the meetings you attend through meeting_users
      instead of joining through users.
    - Create and update both check the ids before changing anything. A bad id
      therefore leaves no half-created meeting in the session, and an update
      with a bad id changes nothing, including the title.

  New tests
  - The attendee check appears as a users.missing_ids call routed through the
    transport.
  - A failed create leaves nothing behind for the next commit on the same
    session.
  - Duplicate attendee ids are stored once.
  - An update replaces the attendee list.
  - An update with an unknown attendee changes nothing.

  One place meetings still touch user data: meeting responses include each
  attendee's name and email. Those are still loaded through SQLAlchemy's
  attendees relationship, which reads the users table, not through the mesh.
  That's why meetings.get/list declare reads=("users",) for the cache. Fully
  separating them would mean storing only attendee ids and fetching each user
  via mesh.users.get, which costs one call per attendee (mostly cached) and
  needs a lookup that doesn't apply the visibility rule. It's doable, but I'd
  call this out in the README as a deliberate choice rather than change it.

  That covers the code items. Next is the README rewrite. Do you want me to go
  ahead with it?

